I see the issue. In the `cbytes < 0` case, when `token & 0x1` is false (i.e., token is even), the code does nothing - it doesn't set `nbytes` or `_dest`. Then `_dest += nbytes` uses uninitialized `nbytes`. Let me look more carefully.

Looking at the code:
```c
else if (cbytes < 0) {
  // A negative number means some encoding depending on the token that comes next
  uint8_t token = src[0];
  src += 1;
  ctbytes += 1;
  if (token & 0x1) {
    // A run of bytes that are different than 0
    if (cbytes < -255) {
      // Runs can only encode a byte
      return -2;
    }
    uint8_t value = -cbytes;
    memset(_dest, value, (unsigned int) neblock);
    nbytes = neblock;
    cbytes = 0;  // everything is encoded in the cbytes token
  }
}
```

If `token & 0x1` is false (token is even), then `nbytes` is never set, and `_dest += nbytes` uses uninitialized `nbytes`. This is the use-of-uninitialized-value bug.

Let me check the git history to see if there's a fix for this. Let me look at the actual fix commit in the upstream repo.
